## Phase 5 — Robots `max-snippet` / `max-video-preview` `'none'` spec fix (Finding 4)

**Why:** Per Google's robots-meta-tag spec, `max-snippet` and `max-video-preview` accept only
an integer (`0` = "none", `-1` = "let Google choose"); only `max-image-preview` accepts the
literal `none`. `robotsObjectToString` (`src/utils/robots.ts:80-90`) interpolates the value
verbatim, so `{ maxSnippet: 'none' }` emits the invalid `max-snippet:none` and
`{ maxVideoPreview: 'none' }` emits `max-video-preview:none`; Google ignores an unparseable
directive, silently defeating the author's intent. The type unions include `'none'`
(`src/types.ts:240,254`) and existing tests lock in the wrong output.

**Tasks:**
1. In `robotsObjectToString` (`src/utils/robots.ts`), map `'none'` → `0` (the spec-correct
   "no snippet" / "static image only") for these two fields, keeping backward-compat with
   callers who pass `'none'`:
   ```ts
   if (opt.maxSnippet !== undefined) {
     // Google: max-snippet takes an integer; `0` means "no snippet". The
     // legacy `'none'` value maps to `0` (emitting `max-snippet:none` is
     // invalid and Google would ignore it).
     const v = opt.maxSnippet === 'none' ? 0 : opt.maxSnippet;
     parts.push(`max-snippet:${v}`);
   }
   // ...
   if (opt.maxVideoPreview !== undefined) {
     // Google: max-video-preview takes an integer; `0` means "static image
     // only" (no video preview). Legacy `'none'` maps to `0`.
     const v = opt.maxVideoPreview === 'none' ? 0 : opt.maxVideoPreview;
     parts.push(`max-video-preview:${v}`);
   }
   ```
   Leave `maxImagePreview` unchanged (it legitimately accepts `none`/`standard`/`large`).
2. Update `src/types.ts` JSDoc for `maxSnippet` and `maxVideoPreview`: document that `'none'`
   is accepted for backward-compat and serializes to the spec-correct `:0`. Update the
   **existing** robots tests that assert `max-snippet:none` / `max-video-preview:none`
   (`tests/utils/robots.test.ts:53-56,201-204,283-287,305-312`) to expect `:0`, and add
   explicit new tests for the `'none' → :0` mapping (top-level and `googlebot`).
3. Update the README "Robots Directives" note if it implies `none` is valid for these fields.
   Add a `CHANGELOG.md` `[Unreleased]` **Fixed** entry (file paths).

---

## Phase 6 — Two-instance shared-tag ownership (Finding 5)

**Why:** `updateMetaInternal` (`src/useSEO.ts:276-289`) / `updateLinkInternal`
(`:402-425`) track the returned element in `addedElements.current` whenever it carries the
SEO marker. `getOrCreateMeta`/`getOrCreateLink` return an **existing** marked element created
by **another** instance. So instance B, writing the same single-value tag, adopts instance A's
element into B's tracking Set; B's `clearSEOTags()` (or B unmounting with
`clearOnUnmount: true`) then deletes A's element, and A cannot recover (its `prevConfigRef` is
unchanged). Trigger is non-default (two live instances on the same tag + one opting into
removal), hence low-medium — but it is a genuine ownership defect that contradicts the
"avoids interfering with other components' tags" design note.

**Primary approach (preferred):** Track **only elements this instance actually created**, not
pre-existing marked ones.
- In `src/utils/dom.ts`, make `getOrCreateMeta` and `getOrCreateLink` add the **newly created**
  element to the passed `trackedElements` set on their create path (they already receive the
  set for dedup removal). Reused/adopted elements are not added.
- In `src/useSEO.ts`, drop the marker-based `addedElements.current.add(...)` in
  `updateMetaInternal`/`updateLinkInternal` (tracking is now owned by the create path).
- Same-instance re-render is unaffected (the element was added on its creation render and stays
  in the Set); cross-instance adoption no longer happens.

**Tasks:**
1. Implement the primary approach. Re-read `getOrCreateMeta`/`getOrCreateLink`/`createMeta`,
   `ensureEssentialMeta`, and every call site; trace the multi-value (`createMeta`) and
   essential-meta paths (unaffected — they add directly). Update the JSDoc of the two helpers
   to document the "adds newly-created elements to `trackedElements`" contract. If two
   sandboxed judges find this fix regressive against the existing tracking tests, fall back to
   the **documentation-only** approach: document in README + `clearSEOTags`/`clearOnUnmount`
   JSDoc that shared single-value tags are last-writer-owned and cleanup may remove a tag a
   sibling live instance depends on — and record the judges' reasoning.
2. **Tests**: (a) a two-instance test — instance A `{ description:'A' }`, instance B
   `{ description:'B' }`, then B `clearSEOTags()` must **not** remove A's still-needed
   `meta[name="description"]`; (b) same for a shared `link` (e.g. `canonical`); (c) adjust
   `tests/utils/dom.test.ts` for the new create-path tracking behavior; (d) re-confirm the
   existing single-instance tracking/`clearSEOTags` tests still pass unchanged.
3. Add a `CHANGELOG.md` `[Unreleased]` **Fixed** entry (file paths); note the chosen approach.

---

## Phase 7 — `twitter:player` HTTPS dev-warning (Finding 6)

**Why:** `twitter:player` emission (`src/useSEO.ts:1104`) gates only on generic URL validity,
so `http://…` player URLs pass through even though the type JSDoc (`src/types.ts:943`) says
"HTTPS-only" and X rejects non-HTTPS player URLs. Consistent with the library's
**warn-don't-block** philosophy (it already warns rather than silently altering values), the
fix is a **dev-mode warning** — not a skip (skipping would be inconsistent with how `og:url`/
`og:image` emit `http:` values).

**Tasks:**
1. In `src/useSEO.ts`, when `twitterPlayer` (and `twitterPlayerStream`) is emitted and the
   value does not start with `https:`, call `warn(...)` (e.g. `twitter:player should be an
   HTTPS URL; X rejects non-HTTPS player URLs`) guarded by `enableWarnings`. Still emit the
   tag (no behavior change to the DOM output). Do **not** change the skip logic.
2. **Tests** (Twitter Player block): with `NODE_ENV=development`, `enableWarnings: true`, and
   an `http:` `twitterPlayer`, assert the warning fires and the tag is still emitted; with an
   `https:` value assert no such warning. Mirror for `twitterPlayerStream`.
3. Update the README "Twitter Player Card" section to state the URLs must be HTTPS (a dev
   warning fires otherwise). Add a `CHANGELOG.md` `[Unreleased]` entry (file paths).

